event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_00ca332799e32113006a5f8a8c833881968dbed784dec13af1","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":329,"text":"The latest updates on NASA’s Mars Perseverance rover include:\n\n- **Perseverance has completed a “marathon” on Mars**: NASA reports the rover has driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across the Martian surface, reaching that milestone on its **1,890th Martian day, or sol**. It did so in about **5 years and 4 months**, much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE image captured the rover and its tracks from orbit just before the milestone【5:0†source】.\n\n- **New science from Jezero Crater’s rim**: NASA’s recent Perseverance news highlights that the rover has been investigating a rock sequence called the **“Broom Point member”** on the rim of Jezero Crater. Scientists say this roughly **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock appears to record **repeated asteroid impacts**, offering insight into early Martian geologic history【5:1†source】.\n\n- **Recent imagery and route updates**: NASA also published updates showing Perseverance’s route to **Broom Point** and a sweeping view looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater, taken by the rover’s Mastcam-Z instrument【5:1†source】.\n\nIn short: the biggest recent headline is that **Perseverance has now driven a marathon distance on Mars**, while continuing detailed science work beyond Jezero Crater’s rim, especially around ancient impact-related rocks at Broom Point."}

